== Mathematical Origin ==

=== The Bistable Potential ===

The nonlinear ψ field has an effective potential:

:<math>V(\psi) = \frac{1}{2}m_{\rm eff}^2\psi^2 + \frac{\lambda}{4}\psi^4</math>

For <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 > 0</math> (normal regime): <math>V</math> has a single minimum at <math>\psi = 0</math> — only one stable state. For <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 < 0</math> (tachyonic regime, requires the nonlinear mass shift to dominate): <math>V</math> is a double-well with two minima:

:<math>\psi_{\rm stable} = 0 \quad \text{(unstable for } m_{\rm eff}^2 < 0\text{)} \quad\text{and}\quad \psi_{\rm sol} = \sqrt{\frac{-m_{\rm eff}^2}{\lambda}}</math>

The soliton amplitude <math>\psi_{\rm sol}</math> and zero are both stable states — bistability. The potential barrier height:

:<math>\Delta V = \frac{m_{\rm eff}^4}{4\lambda}</math>

For thermal stability, the barrier must exceed <math>k_BT</math>: <math>m_{\rm eff}^4/(4\lambda) \gg k_BT</math>. This places a constraint on <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> and <math>\lambda</math> that is a TML-3 measurement target.

=== Condition for Negative m²_eff ===

The effective mass squared inside a material (from [[ψ Constitutive Relations]] B1):

:<math>m_{\rm eff,mat}^2 = \varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi\,m_{\rm eff,vac}^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math>

For <math>m_{\rm eff,mat}^2 < 0</math>: this requires <math>m_{\rm eff,vac}^2 < 0</math> (tachyonic vacuum ψ field) — a more exotic condition than anything in the current framework. Alternatively, the local effective mass can be driven negative by a large negative contribution from material coupling:

:<math>m_{\rm eff,mat}^2 = m_{\rm vac}^2 - |\delta m^2_{\rm material}|</math>

if the material contribution is negative and larger than the vacuum mass squared. This requires a special material class — a ψ metamaterial analogue of a superconducting condensate where the material acts as a ψ vacuum. This is deep Track B speculation; it is included here as the mathematical specification for what such a material would need to achieve.

=== Retention Time ===

In the bistable regime, the soliton state ψ_sol decays toward ψ = 0 due to material loss (tan δ_ψ). The decay time constant:

:<math>\tau_{\rm ret} = \frac{2Q_\psi}{\omega_0}</math>

For hour-scale retention (<math>\tau_{\rm ret} = 3600</math> s) at 40 kHz:

:<math>Q_\psi > \frac{\omega_0 \tau_{\rm ret}}{2} = \frac{2\pi \times 4\times10^4 \times 3600}{2} \approx 4.5\times10^8</math>

This is Q_ψ > 10⁸ for hour-scale retention at 40 kHz — or equivalently, tan δ_ψ < 10⁻⁸. No room-temperature material achieves this. The practical approach for near-term research is to use minute-scale retention (Q ~ 10⁶, achievable with future low-temperature composites) for short-term ψ state buffering in [[PsiNet]] synchronisation applications.

=== Write and Read Protocols ===

'''Write (0 → ψ_sol):''' Apply a ψ pulse at the [[ψ Amplifier]] (C7) gain frequency for duration <math>5\tau_{\rm establish} = 5 \times 2Q/\omega</math>. The field builds to ψ_sol amplitude, then sustains itself in the soliton well. Remove pump.

'''Read:''' Couple the cavity weakly to a [[ψ Detector]] (C10) bifilar probe at coupling Q_coup >> Q_mat. The transmitted back-EMF amplitude distinguishes ψ_sol (logical 1) from ψ = 0 (logical 0). Non-destructive read: coupling must be weak enough that the read perturbation does not push the system over the potential barrier.

'''Erase (ψ_sol → 0):''' Apply a phase-inverted ψ pulse that destructively interferes with the stored soliton, reducing it below the potential barrier. The system then relaxes to ψ = 0.

